# Frontend Auth & Routing

## Session (`common-ui`)

| Piece | Path | Role |
|-------|------|------|
| `AuthSignal` | `auth/auth.signal.ts` | Readonly signal + localStorage session |
| `logIn` / `logOut` / `updateAuth` | same file | Mutate session |
| `authInterceptor` | `auth/http-auth.interceptor.ts` | Bearer token; refresh on 401 |
| `isLoggedIn` | `auth/is-logged-in.guard.ts` | Redirect unauthenticated users |
| `hasRole` | `auth/has-role.guard.ts` | Check `route.data.roles` |

Register `authInterceptor` in each app's `app.config.ts`.

## Admin UI route pattern

Parent feature routes in `app.routes.ts`:

```ts
{
  path: 'users',
  component: LayoutComponent,
  data: { roles: [UserRoleEnum.Admin] },
  canActivate: [isLoggedIn, hasRole],
  canActivateChild: [isLoggedIn, hasRole],
  loadChildren: () => import('./features/users/users.routes').then(m => m.routes),
}
```

Child routes repeat `data.roles` and `title` per screen. Public auth routes under `/auth` — no guards.

## Rules

- Role values from `UserRoleEnum` in `@app/contracts` — not string literals.
- After login, redirect by role (see `LoginComponent` — default `/dashboard`).
- Use `AuthSignal()` in components for current user; extend `BaseComponent` for shared auth helpers.
- Do not add a second token store or HTTP interceptor — extend the existing auth layer.

## user-ui

Minimal shell today — when adding auth, reuse `common-ui` guards/interceptor; do not fork auth state.
